**The vulnerable handler**, which is what most endpoints look like before the second user exists:

```js
app.get('/timesheets/:id', async (request, reply) => {
  const timesheet = await db.oneOrNone('SELECT * FROM timesheets WHERE id = $1', [request.params.id]);
  if (!timesheet) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'not_found' });
  return reply.send(timesheet);
});
```

Any signed-in contractor reads any timesheet by changing the number in the URL.

**A fix that works but must be repeated everywhere:**

```js
  const timesheet = await db.oneOrNone('SELECT * FROM timesheets WHERE id = $1', [request.params.id]);
  if (!timesheet) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'not_found' });
  if (timesheet.ownerId !== request.session.user.id) {
    return reply.code(403).send({ error: 'forbidden' });
  }
```

**The fix that scopes the question**, so the row is never loaded at all:

```js
// repository — the ONLY way handlers read a timesheet
async function findOwnedTimesheet(id, ownerId) {
  return db.oneOrNone(
    'SELECT * FROM timesheets WHERE id = $1 AND owner_id = $2',
    [id, ownerId],
  );
}

app.get('/timesheets/:id', async (request, reply) => {
  const timesheet = await findOwnedTimesheet(request.params.id, request.session.user.id);
  if (!timesheet) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'not_found' });
  return reply.send(timesheet);
});
```

The owner id is read from `request.session.user.id` and from nowhere else. Note what happens if a future contributor forgets a check in some new handler that uses `findOwnedTimesheet`: nothing, because the scope is inside the query.

**The regression test that would have failed before the fix:**

```js
test('a contractor cannot read another contractor timesheet', async () => {
  const alice = await signIn('alice@larkfield.test');
  const bobSheet = await seedTimesheet({ ownerEmail: 'bob@larkfield.test', hours: 37 });

  const response = await alice.get(`/timesheets/${bobSheet.id}`);

  expect(response.status).toBe(404);
  expect(JSON.stringify(response.body)).not.toContain('37');   // no leaked field
});
```

The second assertion is the one that earns its place. A handler that replies with a refusal status *and* the record in the body would pass a status-only test while still leaking every field.

items.promptMarkdown:
This Larkfield handler was written in response to a security review, and it does contain an ownership check. A penetration tester still reads other contractors' timesheets through it.

```js
app.patch('/timesheets/:id', async (request, reply) => {
  const user = request.session?.user;
  if (!user) return reply.code(401).send({ error: 'authentication_required' });

  const { ownerId, hours } = request.body;

  const timesheet = await db.oneOrNone(
    'SELECT * FROM timesheets WHERE id = $1',
    [request.params.id],
  );
  if (!timesheet) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'not_found' });

  if (timesheet.ownerId !== ownerId) {
    return reply.code(403).send({ error: 'forbidden' });
  }

  await db.none('UPDATE timesheets SET hours = $1 WHERE id = $2', [hours, timesheet.id]);
  return reply.send({ ...timesheet, hours });
});
```

Why does the check fail to stop the tester?

solution.explanationMarkdown:
The comparison looks like an authorisation check and is really a self-consistency check. The attacker supplies both a path parameter naming somebody else's timesheet and a body field naming that timesheet's owner; the two agree, so the handler proceeds.

The rule this violates is simple to state and easy to forget under time pressure: **one side of an ownership comparison must come from the session and never from the request.** Anything the client sent is an input to be validated, not a fact to be trusted.

The minimal fix is `if (timesheet.ownerId !== user.id)`. The better fix removes the possibility of forgetting:

```js
const timesheet = await db.oneOrNone(
  'SELECT * FROM timesheets WHERE id = $1 AND owner_id = $2',
  [request.params.id, user.id],
);
if (!timesheet) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'not_found' });
```

Now another contractor's timesheet is indistinguishable from one that does not exist, and the `ownerId` field in the body can be dropped entirely — a field a client can send is a field somebody will eventually try to abuse.

solution.code:
function scopedIds(entries, viewerId, requestedIds) {
  const owned = new Set();
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry.ownerId === viewerId) owned.add(entry.id);
  }
  const seen = new Set();
  const result = [];
  for (const id of requestedIds) {
    if (owned.has(id) && !seen.has(id)) {
      seen.add(id);
      result.push(id);
    }
  }
  return result;
}

grading.starterCode:
function scopedIds(entries, viewerId, requestedIds) {
  // Work out which ids this viewer owns, then keep the requested ones.
  return requestedIds;
}

items.promptMarkdown:
An access bug is fixed: `GET /timesheets/:id` used to return any record, and now scopes the query by owner. The pull request adds this test.

```js
test('rejects a timesheet the caller does not own', async () => {
  const alice = await signIn('alice@larkfield.test');
  const response = await alice.get('/timesheets/999999');

  expect(response.status).toBe(404);
});
```

Id `999999` does not exist in the test database. Would this test have failed against the vulnerable version of the handler, and what would you change?

solution.explanationMarkdown:
The test exercises the *missing record* path, not the *someone else's record* path. Those are the same response by design after the fix, which is precisely why the test cannot tell the two versions apart. A regression test that passes against the vulnerable code is worse than no test, because it converts an open question into a false reassurance in the review.

The corrected version needs two things:

```js
test('a contractor cannot read another contractor timesheet', async () => {
  const bobSheet = await seedTimesheet({ ownerEmail: 'bob@larkfield.test', hours: 37, note: 'site survey' });
  const alice = await signIn('alice@larkfield.test');

  const response = await alice.get(`/timesheets/${bobSheet.id}`);

  expect(response.status).toBe(404);
  expect(JSON.stringify(response.body)).not.toContain('site survey');
});
```

First, a record that really exists and really belongs to Bob — that is the only input that distinguishes the two versions of the handler. Second, an assertion about the *body*. A status-only assertion would still pass against a handler that returns a refusal with the record attached, which is a real and common way for a fix to be incomplete.

Keeping the original id-`999999` case is fine as a separate test of the not-found path; it just should not be labelled as the security regression.

